# ── 2. Fallback: Broadcom upstream tarball ──────────────────────────────────
# Used only if the OEM deb cannot be downloaded. This is the authoritative
# upstream that Canonical itself packages from (recorded in the packaging
# repository's debian/watch). It is also where new driver versions appear
# first, so it is the place to watch for future updates.
Filename:  brcm_linux_fp_5.15.285_5.15.010.0.tgz
Size:      11727635 bytes
SHA256:    67dc8dd47a58e0f9005a42b9bb7048345831257bfd28f34ddd0db1ef07a0e9bc

#   https://packages.broadcom.com/artifactory/dell-controlvault-drivers/brcm_linux_fp_5.15.285_5.15.010.0.tgz
#
# The tarball lays the same driver .so, udev rule, and firmware as the deb, but
# it carries no firmware-settle helper (update-fw.py). Broadcom's own installer
# notes that firmware "will be automatically verified on device access", so the
# driver settles its firmware on first use; the deb's proactive step is a
# Canonical packaging addition, not a requirement.

# ── Manual install when the network is awkward ──────────────────────────────
#   OEM deb:   sudo ./install.sh --deb /path/to/the.deb
#   Upstream:  sudo ./install.sh --tarball /path/to/brcm_linux_fp_*.tgz
#   Verify first:  sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS   (run from this directory)

# Robustness upgrade (deferred): the authoritative SHA256 for the OEM deb is
# also carried in Canonical's GPG-signed Packages index for the OEM pocket. A
# future install.sh could verify the signed index and read the hash from there,
# rather than trusting the hash committed in SHA256SUMS. For now the committed
# hashes are the trust anchor, reviewed in git, matching the AUR/Fedora/Gentoo
# precedent.
